    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Technical sketch · System architecture</p>
      <h1 id="article-title">The carrier as an architecture model.</h1>
      <p>
        For a system architect, Marici's carrier resembles a typed architecture description:
        topology, interfaces, operating conditions, transitions, and the rules that make composed
        operations coherent.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p><a href="/guides/carrier-and-algebra/">← Return to the general explainer</a></p>

      <h2>The shortest useful model</h2>
      <pre><code>{`Marici ≈
    derived relational store
  + typed sector database view
  + coherent transport runtime
  + physical UI view`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The store contains identities, labelled occurrences, typed relations, strata, and
        provenance. A sector view selects the relevant coefficient objects and legal operations.
        Transport preserves or explicitly corrects that view across gluing, specialization,
        continuation, and chart changes. The physical UI view supplies effects, support,
        normalization, and the final observable record.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a literal database ontology. In particular, stored decomposition is not
        operational distinguishability. Two rows or summands count as distinct physical outcomes
        only when a source-derived effect can distinguish them. Entropy measures unresolved
        multiplicity in the rendered physical view—not table size.
      </p>

      <h2>Datomic: identity, immutable value, and history</h2>
      <p>
        Datomic is a closer analogy than a mutable SQL database because it separates enduring
        identity from immutable database values and retains transaction provenance. A rough
        correspondence is:
      </p>
      <pre><code>Datomic entity identity  ↔ labelled physical identity
immutable database value ↔ one coherent sector/context view
transaction history      ↔ provenance of admitted transformations
query                     ↔ sector lens or physical readout</code></pre>
      <p>
        But the final line cannot be pushed too far. A Datomic database value is an ordinary
        commutative collection of facts: its datoms can in principle participate in one global
        query. Marici’s exact scattering and flavor controls instead contain internally
        commutative outcome views whose projectors do not commute across contexts. Two valid views
        need not possess one physical join that preserves both as simultaneous facts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bounded analogy is therefore <strong>Datomic-like immutable identity and
        provenance, generalized by noncommuting contextual materializations</strong>. A quantum
        context is not merely an <code>as-of</code> database time, and Gauss–Manin or Gysin
        transport is not ordinary transaction history.
      </p>

      <h2>Not just readout: a read/write interaction surface</h2>
      <p>
        A UI contains queries and commands. The physical analogue does too. Read-only effects
        return records; preparations, channels, detector choices, and postselection are authorized
        writes that change what later interactions can observe. The proper object is an instrument:
        one operation emits an outcome and produces a conditional next state.
      </p>
      <pre><code>current state
    → capability-authorized interaction
    → (public event record, conditional next state)</code></pre>
      <p>
        Public UI, developer tools, and server internals should also remain distinct. A stronger
        apparatus can expose diagnostic data or a side channel that is absent from the ordinary
        public contract. Such data can be observable without being a stable semantic property of
        the underlying object.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distinction is mathematically consequential, not cosmetic. Formal instrument
        completions show that a direct coarse interaction and a fine diagnostic measurement
        followed by forgetting can report the same coarse probability while producing different
        backaction: the diagnostic path can erase coherence preserved by the coarse path.
        Inspecting the “network tab” may change the system. Current scattering and flavor sources
        do not yet select these update maps, so the physical instrument layer remains conjectural.
      </p>

      <h2>Architecture, not workload</h2>
      <p>
        The carrier is not the running application or its current data. It is the substrate on
        which several applications could run: components, typed ports, boundary conditions,
        intersections of conditions, legal transitions, and consistency requirements.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`struct Carrier {
    components: Vec<Component>,
    interfaces: Vec<Occurrence<Interface>>,
    strata: Vec<OperatingStratum>,
    incidence: Vec<Incidence>,
    transitions: Vec<Transition>,
    coherence: Vec<CoherenceRule>,
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Labelled occurrences are typed ports</h2>
      <p>
        Two ports may implement the same coarse contract and carry identical bytes without being
        interchangeable. <code>Checkout.PaymentOut</code> and <code>Ledger.PaymentIn</code> both
        carry a payment, but one is a producer occurrence and the other a consumer occurrence.
        Their component, side, direction, protocol, and orientation are part of their type.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`struct EndpointOccurrence<T> {
    component: ComponentId,
    port: PortId,
    direction: Direction,
    protocol: Protocol,
    payload: T,
    coarse_contract: ContractId,
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Connecting them is an explicit architectural operation. Equality of payloads does not by
        itself authorize the connection.
      </p>

      <h2>Strata are operating contexts</h2>
      <p>
        A carrier records normal operation, each declared boundary condition, and their
        intersections. For example:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`S₀  normal operation
S₁  database unavailable
S₂  payment provider unavailable
S₁₂ both unavailable`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The corner <code>S₁₂</code> need not be determined by handling <code>S₁</code> and
        <code>S₂</code> independently. Recovery, ordering, or consistency rules may interact only
        when both conditions hold. In Marici, that corner data belongs to the carrier rather than
        being an afterthought.
      </p>

      <h2>The carrier algebra is the operation catalogue</h2>
      <p>
        Restriction, residue, gluing, specialization, pushforward, and pullback are analogous to
        typed architecture transformations. Every operation has a declared source and target.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`enum CarrierOperation {
    Restrict { from: StratumId, to: StratumId },
    Residue { along: BoundaryId },
    Glue { occurrences: Vec<OccurrenceId>, target: CoarseObjectId },
    Specialize { normal: NormalDirectionId, order: usize },
    PushForward { map: ArchitectureMapId },
    PullBack { map: ArchitectureMapId },
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Coherence is composition testing</h2>
      <p>
        A mature architecture tests more than isolated operations. It asks whether two routes
        through the system agree:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`restrict → glue
     ≅
glue → restrict`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Sometimes they agree strictly. Sometimes their difference is a required corner correction.
        This resembles transaction, retry, replication, and failover consistency: local correctness
        does not guarantee compositional correctness.
      </p>

      <h2>Several sectors, one architectural substrate</h2>
      <pre><code>{`struct Sector<Coeff, Response, Record> {
    carrier: Carrier,
    calculus: CarrierAlgebra,
    coefficients: Coeff,
    response: Response,
    pairing: Pairing<Coeff, Response, Record>,
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Different sectors may share the topology and operation types while transporting different
        pairs of physical objects. One side may be a coefficient system, detector, or differential
        form; the other may be an integration cycle, memory field, or response object. The
        observable is produced by their typed pairing, not necessarily by either object alone.
      </p>

      <h2>The readout behaves like a bidirectional adapter</h2>
      <p>
        A useful software analogy is a request/response boundary whose two sides have opposite
        variance. If an architecture map <code>q: G → H</code> forgets detail, coefficient data
        may be pulled back from <code>H</code> to <code>G</code>, while response or cycle data is
        pushed forward from <code>G</code> to <code>H</code>. A valid pairing obeys an adjunction:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`pair_G(pull_back(q, coefficient), response)
    ==
pair_H(coefficient, push_forward(q, response))`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        This is stricter than saying that every sector has a “lens” followed by a readout. The two
        sides form one typed interaction contract. Transporting only one side can change the
        observable even when both individual transformations look legal.
      </p>

      <h2>Capability is not authority</h2>
      <p>
        The generic calculus may know how a pushforward should behave algebraically. That does not
        authorize every sector to use it. A physical pushforward also needs a source-defined map,
        orientation, boundary behavior, support rule, and normalization. In architecture terms:
        implementing an adapter interface does not grant a service permission to call it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current exact controls exhibit the paired pattern in three different settings: a
        cosmological coefficient system paired with a chamber cycle, a string differential form
        paired with an oriented integration chamber, and a gravitational-memory field paired with
        its supertranslation detector. Their payload types differ; the shared candidate is the
        variance-sensitive interaction and coherence calculus.
      </p>

      <h2>A worked proof-of-concept</h2>
      <p>
        The five-site infinity calculation now realizes the layers separately in one exact,
        source-normalized case:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`carrier:
    blow_up(two_normals: (1 / total_energy, 1 / loop_radius))

coefficient:
    deepest_exceptional_grade
    + odd_character_under_global_sheet_flip

response:
    positive_euclidean_chamber

record:
    pair(coefficient, response)
    == exact_mixed_tate_period_C5`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The same exceptional divisor also carries shallower, even coefficient grades. They are not
        additional carrier components and cannot be identified with the odd physical coefficient.
        The chamber supplies the physical evaluation; it is not recoverable by choosing an
        algebraic basis for the coefficient line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Specialization retypes symmetry. Only identity and global sheet complement preserve one
        exceptional grade, but the other 30 sheet changes are not lost: they act as exact
        correspondences between differently shifted fractional lattices. The response lattices
        shift in the opposite direction, so coefficient poles cancel response zeros and the paired
        record is unchanged. In architecture terms, entering a mode can turn an in-place adapter
        into a typed migration between interface versions while preserving end-to-end behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        This proof-of-concept is deliberately narrow: it is established for the coalesced-focus
        five-cycle current. The generic asymmetric family and the shallower coefficient objects
        remain open.
      </p>

      <h2>A second proof-of-concept: the adapter alone is not the readout</h2>
      <p>
        A mass-insertion corner now gives a smaller exact example in which every layer can be
        inspected. Two existing total-energy boundaries meet in the carrier. Their supported
        coefficient object has two channels, exchanged by a sign/deck involution:
      </p>
      <pre><code>carrier:
    intersection(total_energy_left, total_energy_right)

coefficient lens:
    channels A = (y1 + y2)^2, B = (y1 - y2)^2
    deck action swaps A and B

scalar adapter:
    s = A + B, p = A * B
    first two records reconstruct the unordered pair

response:
    two small-pole cycles exchanged by the same deck action

physical record:
    invariant cycle trace survives
    anti-invariant cycle trace is annihilated</code></pre>
      <p>
        This example separates three mechanisms that can look identical from a final scalar.
        The map from the two-channel lens to <code>(s, p)</code> is a smooth finite quotient: it
        forgets channel ordering but no continuous data. The physical equal-mass source reaches
        that quotient tangentially, because its first visible normal coordinate is
        <code>(y1 - y2)^2</code>. Source ramification and readout information loss therefore occur
        in different arrows.
      </p>
      <p>
        The coefficient connection is flat and explicitly pure gauge, so it has no nontrivial
        local monodromy of its own. The response object is different: looping around the invariant
        mass-difference coordinate exchanges its two small-pole cycles. Their source residues are
        equal, so the pairing keeps the invariant trace, kills the anti-trace, and limits to the
        central physical pole when the cycles collide.
      </p>
      <p>
        In architecture language, a stateless adapter can be completely trivializable while the
        end-to-end protocol still has meaningful failover or replica permutation state. The
        observable belongs to the adapter-plus-response contract. Inspecting the adapter alone
        cannot recover which response trace is authorized.
      </p>

      <h2>A third proof-of-concept: structure permits, coefficients realize, readout activates</h2>
      <p>
        Exact one-loop photon scattering now provides a case where the architecture produces a
        quantitative physical threshold rather than only a type distinction. The Carrier and Ward
        constraints construct a rank-two photon coefficient fiber, but they do not select the QED
        ray inside it. QED supplies that sector-specific coefficient object; a frozen Bell protocol
        supplies the physical readout.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`carrier/calculus:
    four-photon crossing + Ward fiber + helicity transport

coefficient object:
    exact massive-electron one-loop helicity amplitude
    (Phi1, Phi2, Phi5)

physical readout:
    maximally-entangled input
    + fixed CHSH analyzers
    + normalized outgoing-helicity pairing

record:
    abs(Bell(s, theta))

derived transverse threshold:
    s / m_e^2 = 0.4201576087546...
    sqrt(s) / m_e = 0.6481956562...`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The threshold is neither stored in the Carrier nor fitted into the readout. Two consecutive
        EFT jets predict it, and the full massive one-loop amplitude confirms it below the
        electron-pair threshold. Independent precision runs reproduce the root. A refined angular
        census places the last sampled nonviolating direction at the transverse point; a continuum
        angular certificate remains open.
      </p>
      <p>
        This separates four layers cleanly: the Carrier supplies structural possibility, the
        coefficient object realizes one sector, the readout states what is physically interrogated,
        and their pairing produces the observable record. Asking the Carrier alone to predict the
        QED coefficient ray would confuse permission with realization. Asking the coefficient object
        alone for a Bell threshold would omit the measurement contract that makes the threshold
        meaningful.
      </p>

      <h2>The revised architecture question</h2>
      <p>
        The research question is now whether the carrier supplies a stable topology of supported
        interactions, while each sector supplies an authorized pair of endpoint objects and the
        physical contract that evaluates them. A failure of a particular payload or detector does
        not refute the shared architecture. A required interaction that cannot be typed by the
        frozen carrier and calculus would.
      </p>
    </div>
